cellFs: serialize the descriptor tables; hand descriptors out round-robin.

Every handler looked an fd up in g_files and then used the FILE* unlocked. Resistance 3's FIOS threads open and close
one descriptor hundreds of times per level while others seek and read on it, and a close landing between the lookup
and the use crashed in ftello on the freed stream. The handlers now hold one recursive lock while they touch the
tables (the AIO completion callback runs outside it). Descriptors are also allocated round-robin instead of
lowest-free, so a late call on a number that was just closed cannot reach a file that reopened it at once.

Upstream #430 (v0.18.0) allocates and releases under a lock and reads each slot once, which keeps a close from
handing a reader NULL; a reader's snapshot can still be fclose'd under it, which is this crash. So the handlers keep
holding g_fs_lock for as long as they use the slot, and allocation (under #430's g_fd_lock) goes round-robin.
v0.19.0's #620 (error codes, EBADF/EMFILE), #641 (cellFsAioWrite) and #645 (cellFsFtruncate) leave the fd table
itself alone, and the two new handlers look their fd up the same unlocked way, so they hold g_fs_lock here too.

Test: tools/test_ppu_fs_fd_table.py (runtime/ppu/tests/test_fs_fd_table.cpp): each handler that uses a slot waits for
g_fs_lock while another thread holds it, the AIO completion callbacks run with it free, and a closed descriptor is
the last to be handed out again (also with the table full).

Upstream: not proposed yet.
---

